WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:03.920
One problem that can come up with switches
is that loops can occur.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.099
They can be physical, such as when a device
is plugged into itself by accident, which

00:00:08.099 --> 00:00:13.270
can cause what is known as a broadcast storm,
in that broadcasts flood through a switch,

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:14.370
eventually overloading it.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.810
If not physical, switches are prone to receiving
multiple copies of the same frame of data

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.980
as it goes through a network.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.890
Switches should have a protocol called Spanning
Tree Protocol enabled to combat this.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:32.810
STP, as it is known, when enabled, uses a
spanning-tree algorithm, known as STA, to

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.780
search for, find, and then destroy redundant
links.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:43.850
Links are found by transmitting BPDUs, Bridge
Protocol Data Units, and then once a redundant

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.879
link is identified, it is destroyed.

